From: Marvis <marvis@local>
Subject: [PATCH] timekeeping: drop WARN_ON(timekeeping_suspended) in ktime_get()

PMICWD (vendor PMIC watchdog) may call ktime_get() while timekeeping
is suspended. The clock is frozen by design in that state, so the
WARN_ON fires on every call, flooding dmesg (observed 7291 times on
SM8850 with a self-built 6.12.69 GKI kernel).

This change only removes the warning line inside ktime_get(). It does
not touch the function signature, the exported symbol, or any KMI
surface; the returned value (frozen clock) is unchanged.

diff --git a/kernel/time/timekeeping.c b/kernel/time/timekeeping.c
--- a/kernel/time/timekeeping.c
+++ b/kernel/time/timekeeping.c
@@ -754,19 +754,18 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL(ktime_get_real_ts64);
 ktime_t ktime_get(void)
 {
 	struct timekeeper *tk = &tk_core.timekeeper;
 	unsigned int seq;
 	ktime_t base;
 	u64 nsecs;
 
-	WARN_ON(timekeeping_suspended);
-
 	do {
 		seq = read_seqcount_begin(&tk_core.seq);
 		base = tk->tkr_mono.base;
 		nsecs = timekeeping_get_ns(&tk->tkr_mono);
 
 	} while (read_seqcount_retry(&tk_core.seq, seq));
 
 	return ktime_add_ns(base, nsecs);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(ktime_get);
